Well, that's nice, but l think anything over $30 billion is just gravy.

490
00:30:41,407 --> 00:30:42,601
l like that.

491
00:30:43,742 --> 00:30:46,609
Your hair is very blond and pretty.

492
00:30:46,712 --> 00:30:49,977
l'm of Swedish ancestry.

493
00:30:50,816 --> 00:30:52,306
-Really? -Yes.

494
00:30:53,252 --> 00:30:56,153
My grandfather was in ABBA.

495
00:30:57,656 --> 00:30:59,248
ABBA, the band?

496
00:30:59,925 --> 00:31:03,725
Excuse me, do you get any famous people in here?

497
00:31:03,829 --> 00:31:05,194
Famous, rich, powerful.

498
00:31:05,297 --> 00:31:08,130
Really? We don't get any famous people in Mandrake Falls.

499
00:31:08,234 --> 00:31:10,702
Stephen King stopped by our gas station one time...

500
00:31:10,803 --> 00:31:13,169
...but everyone was too scared to talk to him.

501
00:31:13,272 --> 00:31:15,035
l'd imagine he'd be very spooky.

502
00:31:15,140 --> 00:31:18,507
Jake, the gas station attendant, made eye contact with him.

503
00:31:18,611 --> 00:31:20,545
That night he lost 200 pounds.

504
00:31:22,781 --> 00:31:23,748
Wow.

505
00:31:24,717 --> 00:31:26,878
Your chicken parmesan should be ready soon.

506
00:31:26,986 --> 00:31:31,184
Okay, buddy. l saw a couple over there. lt's their anniversary.

507
00:31:31,857 --> 00:31:34,621
Give them this. Don't say it's from us, though.

508
00:31:35,394 --> 00:31:36,827
This is like $20,000.

509
00:31:36,929 --> 00:31:40,330
Tell them it was a restaurant giveaway or something. Thanks, buddy.

510
00:31:42,034 --> 00:31:45,435
That's an awfully nice way to start spending your money.

511
00:31:45,537 --> 00:31:47,505
-ls that who l think it is? -Who?

512
00:31:48,173 --> 00:31:51,006
Preston Blake's nephew, the $40-billion man.

513
00:31:51,944 --> 00:31:53,434
We have to meet him. Dave?

514
00:31:54,146 --> 00:31:57,115
Gosh, l hope that mugger got home okay.

515
00:31:57,483 --> 00:31:58,814
-Mr. Deeds. -Yeah.

516
00:31:58,918 --> 00:32:01,910
Mr. Simonds would like to invite you to join his party.

517
00:32:02,521 --> 00:32:03,488
Why is that?

518
00:32:04,089 --> 00:32:06,250
Just to enjoy the pleasure of your company.

519
00:32:06,358 --> 00:32:08,349
Wow, that's pretty cool of him.

520
00:32:12,765 --> 00:32:14,528
That was a very nice hug, Deeds.

521
00:32:15,000 --> 00:32:18,231
lt's wicked nice of you to invite us over. This is awesome.

522
00:32:18,570 --> 00:32:21,596
-Have we met? -Not unless you have a sick kid.

523
00:32:22,074 --> 00:32:25,271
You're a very important man in this town now, Mr. Deeds.

524
00:32:25,377 --> 00:32:26,776
l don't know about that.

525
00:32:26,879 --> 00:32:28,676
l sing at the Metropolitan Opera...

526
00:32:28,781 --> 00:32:32,444
...and Blake Media contributes 50 percent of our budget.

527
00:32:32,551 --> 00:32:34,451
So, on behalf of the opera;

528
00:32:34,553 --> 00:32:38,489
''Thank you''

529
00:32:41,660 --> 00:32:42,957
That was awesome!

530
00:32:43,429 --> 00:32:46,557
You shouldn't thank me, though. You should thank my great-uncle.

531
00:32:46,665 --> 00:32:48,030
He was an interesting man.

532
00:32:48,133 --> 00:32:50,328
l served on the board of the Guggenheim with him.

533
00:32:50,436 --> 00:32:53,337
Really? l'm not sure what that is, but good luck with it.

534
00:32:54,907 --> 00:32:57,705
l'd love to write a piece on you for the New Yorker.

535
00:32:57,810 --> 00:32:59,368
What's the New Yorker?

536
00:33:00,312 --> 00:33:01,404
lt's a magazine.

537
00:33:02,448 --> 00:33:05,110
With essays, witty cartoons.

538
00:33:05,851 --> 00:33:09,787
l read the Mandrake Falls Gazette and they got some cartoons in there also.

539
00:33:09,888 --> 00:33:12,413
You know who's a riot? That Beetle Bailey guy.

540
00:33:12,558 --> 00:33:15,118
He's always wearing his hat and doing his thing.

541
00:33:15,761 --> 00:33:18,525
But it is an honor to meet a real writer like you, George.

542
00:33:18,630 --> 00:33:21,030
l've been trying to get my stuff published for years...

543
00:33:21,133 --> 00:33:23,533
...but Hallmark is one tough cookie to crack.

544
00:33:23,635 --> 00:33:26,160
-Hallmark? -He writes greeting cards.

545
00:33:26,905 --> 00:33:30,068
-l try. -You must share one with us.

546
00:33:30,175 --> 00:33:33,736
-l don't know about that, Mr. French. -Who's Mr. French?

547
00:33:33,846 --> 00:33:37,111
-Doesn't he look like Mr. French? -l've never heard of him.

548
00:33:37,216 --> 00:33:38,148
Come on.

549
00:33:38,250 --> 00:33:39,842
All right, l'll give you one.

550
00:33:39,952 --> 00:33:42,546
This is a Mother's Day card l've been working on.

551
00:33:42,654 --> 00:33:46,715
''Mom, you are the one who brought me to planet Earth

552
00:33:47,626 --> 00:33:50,720
''You are the one who suffered through my 14-hour birth

553
00:33:51,463 --> 00:33:55,399
''You are the one who made lemonade for me after l'd come back from play

554
00:33:55,834 --> 00:33:59,930
''l love you, Mom, so have a wicked nice Mother's Day''

555
00:34:00,472 --> 00:34:01,837
Something like that.

556
00:34:02,674 --> 00:34:05,871
-An instant classic! -''Genius, genius, genius''

557
00:34:06,278 --> 00:34:08,269
Do you write your poems with a crayon?

558
00:34:12,651 --> 00:34:13,879
lt's amazing!

559
00:34:15,254 --> 00:34:17,984
l see why you brought me here. To goof on me, huh?

560
00:34:18,624 --> 00:34:21,115
-Don't be silly, Deeds. -Pipe down, George.

561
00:34:21,860 --> 00:34:24,693
l may seem funny to you, but if you came to Mandrake Falls...

562
00:34:24,797 --> 00:34:27,823
...you might seem funny to us. Only nobody would laugh at you...

563
00:34:27,933 --> 00:34:30,834
...and make you feel stupid, that wouldn't be good manners.

564
00:34:30,936 --> 00:34:34,497
Maybe my poems aren't that great, but l know some people who like them.

565
00:34:34,706 --> 00:34:38,699
Anyways, it's the best l can do. We're going to get going.

566
00:34:38,811 --> 00:34:42,008
Sorry about that, Pam. l hope l didn't embarrass you.

567
00:34:47,052 --> 00:34:50,647
And if it wasn't for Miss Dawson being here, l'd knock your heads in.

568
00:34:50,756 --> 00:34:51,916
l don't mind.

569
00:34:52,257 --> 00:34:53,224
Okay.

570
00:34:56,929 --> 00:34:57,896
''No''

571
00:35:04,603 --> 00:35:08,471
l think l just shat myself.

572
00:35:09,374 --> 00:35:11,740
How would you like to see the sights now?

573
00:35:11,844 --> 00:35:14,335
l just need to powder my nose.

574
00:35:14,446 --> 00:35:16,812
That would be great. l'll get your coat.

575
00:35:17,483 --> 00:35:21,249
Hey, buddy, this will pay for everything l damaged. Sorry about that.

576
00:35:21,353 --> 00:35:23,719
-Good to meet you guys. -Thank you.

577
00:35:23,822 --> 00:35:25,414
God, that was good stuff.

578
00:35:29,027 --> 00:35:30,460
l want a raise, Mac.

579
00:35:34,933 --> 00:35:36,730
Hey, man! What's up?

580
00:35:37,069 --> 00:35:39,435
Holy cannoli, you're John McEnroe!

581
00:35:39,538 --> 00:35:42,564
l saw what you did to those guys making fun of you. Nice work.

582
00:35:42,674 --> 00:35:45,609
You know what it's like to get riled up don't you, Johnny Mac?

583
00:35:45,711 --> 00:35:48,509
-That l do. -l'm Deeds, by the way.

584
00:35:48,614 --> 00:35:52,311
Deeds, nice to meet you. You got to let me buy you a drink.

585
00:35:52,417 --> 00:35:54,180
Miss Dawson, this is John McEnroe.

586
00:35:54,286 --> 00:35:57,915
-Miss Dawson, nice to meet you. -Nice to meet you, too.

587
00:35:58,157 --> 00:36:00,648
l promised Miss Dawson l'd take her sightseeing.

588
00:36:00,759 --> 00:36:03,990
The sights are great. l've seen the sights. l love the sights.

589
00:36:04,096 --> 00:36:07,156
Let me show you a New York most people will never see.

590
00:36:07,666 --> 00:36:11,033
-That okay with you, Miss Dawson? -Sounds like fun!

591
00:36:11,136 --> 00:36:13,570
Here's to a big night on the town.

592
00:36:26,084 --> 00:36:27,711
What happened?

593
00:36:27,853 --> 00:36:32,051
Here are some aspirins, Mr. Deeds. They make your head seem smaller.

594
00:36:36,094 --> 00:36:39,222
-How did l get in these pajamas? -l changed you.

595
00:36:39,798 --> 00:36:41,459
l was very gentle, sir.

596
00:36:42,968 --> 00:36:46,267
l think l drank all the alcohol in Manhattan last night.

597
00:36:46,605 --> 00:36:49,130
And you nearly succeeded, according to Miss Dawson.

598
00:36:50,509 --> 00:36:54,343
Miss Dawson! l don't even remember taking her home. ls she okay?

599
00:36:54,446 --> 00:36:57,472
l took the liberty of sending her off in the company limousine.

600
00:36:57,583 --> 00:37:00,484
She was a lady in distress and l helped her.

601
00:37:01,119 --> 00:37:02,746
Dreamt about her last night.

602
00:37:03,322 --> 00:37:06,052
Usually, when you get blackout drunk you don't dream.

603
00:37:07,659 --> 00:37:08,683
So l read.

604
00:37:13,332 --> 00:37:14,629
What's up, Chuck?

605
00:37:18,170 --> 00:37:20,297
America's newest billionaire...

606
00:37:22,040 --> 00:37:24,065
-...stupid and drunk. -What's this?

607
00:37:24,176 --> 00:37:27,077
It was quite a night for Longfellow Deeds.

608
00:37:27,179 --> 00:37:29,739
They'll know my name is Longfellow.

609
00:37:30,249 --> 00:37:32,581
After punching out some society types...

610
00:37:34,152 --> 00:37:38,521
...Deeds joined forces with the original bad boy of tennis, John McEnroe.

611
00:37:38,857 --> 00:37:41,291
And stupidity won in straight sets.

612
00:37:48,267 --> 00:37:49,962
How'd they get that footage?

613
00:37:50,502 --> 00:37:52,094
Watch out, Johnny!

614
00:37:54,840 --> 00:37:57,968
What kind of driving is that? You're a fucking disgrace!

615
00:37:58,644 --> 00:38:00,612
No, sir. You're a disgrace.

616
00:38:00,712 --> 00:38:03,010
-We did it, man! -Johnny McEnroe!

617
00:38:03,115 --> 00:38:06,414
Look at Deedsy hanging out with McEnroe! That's awesome!

618
00:38:07,586 --> 00:38:09,053
l love the Beach Boys.

619
00:38:09,821 --> 00:38:11,914
Here l go, here l go.

620
00:38:14,693 --> 00:38:17,355
This is not the kind of attention we need right now.

621
00:38:17,562 --> 00:38:18,620
What do you mean?

622
00:38:18,730 --> 00:38:21,563
For the moment you're the largest stockholder...

623
00:38:21,667 --> 00:38:24,192
...and people might think any instability you show...

624
00:38:24,303 --> 00:38:26,430
...may equal instability in the company.

625
00:38:30,542 --> 00:38:31,509
My bad.

626
00:38:33,578 --> 00:38:35,808
My bad. My bad.

627
00:38:35,914 --> 00:38:36,881
Okay. Okay.

628
00:38:36,982 --> 00:38:37,949
-My bad. -Okay.

629
00:38:38,116 --> 00:38:41,279
Shall l call your car, Mr. Cedar, to take you to the funeral?

630
00:38:41,386 --> 00:38:42,683
What funeral?

631
00:38:43,622 --> 00:38:45,249
Your great-uncle's funeral.

632
00:38:45,357 --> 00:38:47,291
-His funeral's today? -Today, yes.

633
00:38:47,693 --> 00:38:49,422
l'll go get my suit on.

634
00:38:56,968 --> 00:38:59,061
-Cute, bigmouth. -Sir?

635
00:38:59,538 --> 00:39:01,802
As soon as that moron goes back to Cowpie Falls...

636
00:39:01,907 --> 00:39:04,603
...you are out of here on your fat Puerto Rican ass.

637
00:39:09,581 --> 00:39:11,276
l hail from Spain, sir.

638
00:39:17,489 --> 00:39:18,683
Ole.

639
00:39:20,125 --> 00:39:24,152
Now, Brother Preston is soaring with eagles high above.

640
00:39:25,464 --> 00:39:28,024
Because he lived a life of love.

641
00:39:29,668 --> 00:39:32,466
Yes, he's flying way up high...

642
00:39:33,472 --> 00:39:35,997
...because he was a supercool guy.

643
00:39:37,376 --> 00:39:40,709
He's gone away, too soon, it seems...

644
00:39:41,380 --> 00:39:44,247
...leaving behind his unfinished dreams.

645
00:39:44,649 --> 00:39:47,345
This guy could make a fortune writing greeting cards.

646
00:39:47,452 --> 00:39:50,250
Yes, we remember Preston Blake...

647
00:39:50,822 --> 00:39:53,620
...a man with faith no man could shake.

648
00:39:54,493 --> 00:39:56,620
A strength no man could break.

649
00:39:57,195 --> 00:39:59,663
A character no man could fake.

650
00:40:00,499 --> 00:40:03,024
For goodness sake, let's eat some cake.

651
00:40:03,435 --> 00:40:04,868
Amen.

652
00:40:06,805 --> 00:40:09,740
Excuse me, Reverend, that was a grand slam.

653
00:40:09,841 --> 00:40:12,742
That part with soaring with the eagles gave me goosebumps.

654
00:40:12,844 --> 00:40:16,336
-Thank you, my brother. -My friend got me a mug for my birthday.

655
00:40:16,448 --> 00:40:20,248
lt says, ''lt's hard to soar with the eagles when you're surrounded by turkeys.''

656
00:40:20,352 --> 00:40:22,047
But what you said was better.

657
00:40:22,487 --> 00:40:23,749
lt's all good.

658
00:40:24,189 --> 00:40:28,057
Rest in peace, Preston. That concludes today's service.

659
00:40:29,628 --> 00:40:31,926
l actually thought l would say something.

660
00:40:33,732 --> 00:40:36,292
Deedsy was always the best speaker in school.

661
00:40:37,102 --> 00:40:37,932
Deedsy?

662
00:40:38,036 --> 00:40:40,163
that's some job, by the way, Your Majesty.

663
00:40:40,539 --> 00:40:42,507
l thought we were watching Scooby-Doo.

664
00:40:43,375 --> 00:40:47,505
Hi, my name is Deeds. l was Preston Blake's great-nephew.

665
00:40:47,946 --> 00:40:51,245
l wrote a poem for him on my way over here, in my head.

666
00:40:51,616 --> 00:40:54,016
And l'm going to recite it for you.

667
00:40:54,719 --> 00:40:58,678
l didn't realize l'd be following the rhyme-master Reverend Sharpton.

668
00:40:59,157 --> 00:41:00,749
But here we go anyways.

669
00:41:01,393 --> 00:41:04,328
''You climbed mountains and built skyscrapers

670
00:41:05,564 --> 00:41:09,432
''You made TV shows and put out newspapers

671
00:41:11,136 --> 00:41:13,696
''You were wicked good at doing stocks

672
00:41:14,873 --> 00:41:17,307
''You liked it when Emilio would change your socks

673
00:41:19,010 --> 00:41:21,774
''We never hung out and that makes me sad

674
00:41:22,681 --> 00:41:24,979
''All the good times we could've had''

675
00:41:25,717 --> 00:41:27,116
Nice rhyme.

676
00:41:27,619 --> 00:41:31,055
''But when l die, Uncle Preston, you'd better say, 'Cheers!'

677
00:41:31,857 --> 00:41:34,485
'''Cause me and you are hanging at the pearly gates

678
00:41:34,593 --> 00:41:38,495
''l'll bring the beers, l'll bring the beers''

679
00:41:39,231 --> 00:41:40,255
He's good.

680
00:41:40,832 --> 00:41:41,821
Amen.

681
00:41:41,933 --> 00:41:45,562
l know this is supposed to be a closed casket, but where l come from...

682
00:41:45,670 --> 00:41:49,299
...we feel it's good to see the body for the grieving process, so....

683
00:41:52,310 --> 00:41:55,279
Sorry about that! l'll keep this sucker closed.

684
00:42:00,085 --> 00:42:01,882
He's still pretty frozen.

685
00:42:02,554 --> 00:42:03,748
Almost there.

686
00:42:06,057 --> 00:42:08,457
l'll keep an eye on the company for you. Love you.

687
00:42:08,560 --> 00:42:10,721
Nice meeting you. Thanks for the apartment.

688
00:42:11,296 --> 00:42:12,456
That was freaky.

689
00:42:12,998 --> 00:42:14,522
Everybody drive home safe.

690
00:42:14,866 --> 00:42:16,925
Man, this guy is in way over his head.

691
00:42:17,035 --> 00:42:19,162
-take care. -You'll see him tonight?

692
00:42:19,271 --> 00:42:22,570
Yes, l'm calling around 4;00. That's when l get off work.

693
00:42:22,841 --> 00:42:28,404
Remember, l'm Pam Dawson, virgin school nurse from Winchestertonfieldville, lowa.

694
00:42:30,882 --> 00:42:32,042
That's priceless.

695
00:42:33,718 --> 00:42:34,776
You, a virgin.

696
00:42:41,493 --> 00:42:43,984
-All right, thank you. -Excuse me, sir.

697
00:42:44,429 --> 00:42:46,829
-l told him he could have that. -Thanks.

698
00:42:46,932 --> 00:42:48,490
Right on. Nice seeing you.

699
00:42:48,800 --> 00:42:51,564
You need to use our bathroom, come back and use it, okay?

700
00:42:51,670 --> 00:42:53,604
-Will do! -All right.

701
00:42:55,106 --> 00:42:57,233
How do you think l did at the funeral today?

702
00:42:57,342 --> 00:42:58,832
You spoke beautifully, sir.

703
00:42:59,311 --> 00:43:00,403
l still feel bad.

704
00:43:00,512 --> 00:43:04,175
l snapped my uncle's hand off when l put him back in the casket.

705
00:43:04,683 --> 00:43:06,583
Lucky for him, he was dead, sir.

706
00:43:07,118 --> 00:43:09,518
Even though his face was frozen that way...

707
00:43:10,055 --> 00:43:12,853
...it looked to me like he was a happy guy. Am l right?

708
00:43:13,391 --> 00:43:16,053
-He truly was. -Come on, sit down, please.

709
00:43:16,595 --> 00:43:20,031
And l got to hang out with him, just like this...

710
00:43:20,565 --> 00:43:22,658
...almost every day for 30 years.

711
00:43:23,201 --> 00:43:24,896
What did you guys talk about?

712
00:43:25,003 --> 00:43:29,133
We spoke of life, love, art, wine, business.

713
00:43:29,574 --> 00:43:31,235
l learned much from him.

714
00:43:31,576 --> 00:43:33,476
-l bet you miss him, too. -l do.

715
00:43:33,912 --> 00:43:35,812
He was like a parent to me.

716
00:43:36,581 --> 00:43:39,641
You see, l never knew my father, and Mama...

717
00:43:40,251 --> 00:43:42,719
...she passed while giving the birth to me.

718
00:43:45,991 --> 00:43:48,221
-You want some Cocoa Pebbles? -No, thanks.

719
00:43:49,160 --> 00:43:52,425
l have much work to do.

720
00:43:52,764 --> 00:43:56,029
-Thanks. You don't have to do that. -Enjoy your Pebbles.

721
00:43:57,435 --> 00:43:58,834
One more thing.

722
00:43:59,471 --> 00:44:02,872
Your great-uncle did not let me change his socks for him, either.

723
00:44:04,042 --> 00:44:05,270
l like feet.

724
00:44:06,277 --> 00:44:07,437
l do not know why.

725
00:44:16,121 --> 00:44:16,951
Hello.

726
00:44:17,055 --> 00:44:19,683
Hi, Deeds? lt's Pam. Pam Dawson.

727
00:44:19,958 --> 00:44:23,951
l'm so happy you called. l'm wicked sorry about last night.

728
00:44:24,062 --> 00:44:25,427
l don't drink very often.

729
00:44:25,730 --> 00:44:28,597
l thought you held your liquor very well.

730
00:44:28,700 --> 00:44:30,998
Unfortunately, l couldn't hold it for too long.

731
00:44:31,202 --> 00:44:34,763
That's okay. l didn't really like that skirt anyway.

732
00:44:36,107 --> 00:44:39,543
l'm getting a call on the other line. Can you hang on for a second?

733
00:44:39,644 --> 00:44:41,271
-Sure. -Thank you.

734
00:44:41,980 --> 00:44:42,776
Hello.

735
00:44:42,881 --> 00:44:45,941
Mr. Deeds, this is Kevin Ward, the quarterback you punched.

736
00:44:46,451 --> 00:44:47,679
What's up, buddy?

